\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig_combined_overview.pdf}
\caption{Overview. (a)~Global correction over/under-corrects subgroups with heterogeneous error rates, introducing bias $\delta_s \cdot \Delta\tau / (1{-}2\bar{\pi})$ that reverses CATE sign in 14.9\% of configurations. (b)~\textit{Top (rose):} standard pipeline applies a pooled confusion matrix uniformly; \textit{bottom (teal):} EC-HTE estimates per-subgroup matrices via hierarchical Bayes, applies stratified inversion with a $\kappa$-guard, and propagates uncertainty.}
\label{fig:method_overview}
\end{figure*}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Consider estimating whether a content moderation policy reduces hate speech differently across demographic groups, using GPT-4 to classify thousands of posts.
After standard confusion-matrix correction, the analysis concludes the policy \emph{increases} hate speech among younger users, but the true effect is protective.
This sign reversal is not an edge case.

Large language models (LLMs) now routinely replace human annotators in computational social science, producing labels for sentiment, hate speech, stance, and political discourse at costs orders of magnitude below manual coding~\cite{gilardi2023chatgpt,tornberg2024chatgpt,ziems2024nlp,tan2024llm}.
When these labels serve as effect modifiers in heterogeneous treatment effect (HTE) analysis, the standard correction protocol\footnote{Throughout, ``debiasing'' refers to correction of annotation measurement-error bias, not algorithmic fairness debiasing.} is straightforward: validate a sample against expert annotations, estimate a confusion matrix, and apply its inverse uniformly to correct conditional average treatment effect (CATE) estimates.
Yet LLM annotations are not uniformly accurate: \citet{ashwin2025bias} document error rates varying by up to 5$\times$ across demographic subgroups; in our pilot, GPT-4.1 error rates range from 10.2\% to 20.0\% across text-feature subgroups ($\Delta\pi = 0.098$).

We show that when error rates vary across subgroups, this standard correction can make things worse.
In 14.9\% of our synthetic configurations ($\tau(0) = 0.03$), global correction reverses the sign of estimated CATE ($\tau = +0.03 \to \hat{\tau} = -0.059$), rising to 31\% at $\tau(0) = 0.01$ (Figure~\ref{fig:sign_reversal}); all eight real LLMs amplify bias, while our per-subgroup correction (EC-HTE; Section~\ref{sec:method}) eliminates sign reversal in the $K{=}2$ setting.
The mechanism is over-correction: pooled correction calibrated for the average error rate introduces substantial bias in low-error subgroups where the naive estimator is nearly unbiased.

We derive an exact bias identity (Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity}), verified at $R^2 \geq 0.97$, whose prescribed per-subgroup correction reduces $|\text{bias}|$ by $7.4\times$ under extreme heterogeneity.
Existing corrections (PPI~\cite{angelopoulos2023ppi}, DSL~\cite{egami2023dsl}, PPCI~\cite{sui2026ppci}, arm-specific PPI~\cite{mozer2026ppi}, ForestIV~\cite{bu2023forestiv}) assume homogeneous prediction error; none addresses effect modifier misclassification (Section~\ref{sec:related}).

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig1_sign_reversal.pdf}
\caption{CATE sign reversal under global correction ($K{=}2$, $\tau(0) = 0.03$). (A) Naive vs.\ global-corrected CATE; red triangles mark sign reversals (7.5\% of subgroup estimates). (B) Extreme case: true $\tau = +0.03$, estimated $\hat{\tau} = -0.059$.}
\label{fig:sign_reversal}
\end{figure*}

Our contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
    \item An exact bias identity (Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity}): global correction introduces subgroup-level bias $\delta_s \cdot \Delta\tau / (1{-}2\bar{\pi})$, verified at $R^2 = 0.975$ across 11 LLM--dataset combinations, with a $\Delta\tau$-free diagnostic (Proposition~\ref{prop:debiasing_hurts}) predicting when correction increases bias from error rates alone.
    \item Empirical operating guidelines: per-subgroup correction outperforms global when $\Delta\pi > 0.15$, $n_{\text{expert}} \geq 250$, and $K \leq 4$.
    \item A concrete instantiation (EC-HTE) using hierarchical Bayesian confusion matrix estimation, verified for DIM, CausalForestDML, and R-learner, composable with PPCI for joint covariate/outcome error.
\end{itemize}

